Selling relay hosting IS Competing Use, because hosted relay is a tier we sell.
Running a relay so that you can do a client's work is not, and never was meant
to be.

WHY FSL RATHER THAN AGPL, BSL OR MIT
------------------------------------

AGPL does not do this job and is banned in much of the market. Section 13 binds
somebody hosting a MODIFIED copy; a competitor running this relay unmodified
would owe nothing and could charge for it. AGPL is reciprocity, not protection
against competing hosting. It is also prohibited outright at some of the
organisations this is built for, and section 13 has no case law to lean on.

BSL is the same shape as FSL with worse ergonomics: per-adopter parameters
rather than one standard text, and a four-year clock rather than two.

MIT protects nothing at all, which was fine while nothing was being sold.

THE HONEST LIMIT, because it is specific to this component
----------------------------------------------------------

FSL's protection is worth what release velocity makes it worth - Sentry
designed it that way, and it cuts badly here. This relay is DELIBERATELY
stable. A two-year-old relay is still a good relay, so the conversion clock
gives a competitor something useful. That is accepted rather than solved: by
the time a competitor has a two-year-old beacon relay, the value has moved to
the beam, the governance plane, and operating the thing.

WHAT IS NOT UNDER THIS LICENCE
------------------------------

